WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:08.097
- So I'm gonna talk about
inference in mathematics.

00:00:08.097 --> 00:00:09.600
I'm a philosopher,

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:12.728
so this will be from a
philosopher's point of view.

00:00:12.728 --> 00:00:15.395
(Windows sound)

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:17.670
I'm pressing the wrong thing.

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:18.840
Ah.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:22.500
So I'm gonna talk about mathematics

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:23.670
and the general theory of inference,

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:25.140
deductive inference in mathematics,

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:27.035
and then non deductive inference.

00:00:27.035 --> 00:00:30.354
So the talk is about
inference in pure mathematics

00:00:30.354 --> 00:00:33.510
and mathematical inference can be,

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:36.120
is unusual in several respects.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:39.480
And it's quite different from,

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:40.800
the talk is quite different

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.890
from the ones you heard this
morning and earlier today

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:46.126
because mathematics is,

00:00:46.126 --> 00:00:50.970
mathematical objects for a
start, they seem to be abstract,

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:55.063
so numbers, functions, sets,
geometric shapes and so on

00:00:55.063 --> 00:00:57.436
that pure mathematics is interested in

00:00:57.436 --> 00:01:02.436
are not spatial temporal,
they're not causal,

00:01:03.086 --> 00:01:05.072
they don't seem to be causal.

00:01:05.072 --> 00:01:07.470
Therefore, mathematics
is not a causal domain.

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:09.420
So any kind of inference
in pure mathematics

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:12.705
is not gonna be a causal inference.

00:01:12.705 --> 00:01:14.928
There are correlations in mathematics,

00:01:14.928 --> 00:01:17.190
but the standard picture

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:19.980
is that all the
correlations are necessary.

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:22.916
You know, if certain spaces
have certain properties,

00:01:22.916 --> 00:01:24.971
well, that's just a necessary fact

00:01:24.971 --> 00:01:27.401
because they couldn't be otherwise.

00:01:27.401 --> 00:01:29.670
And deductive reasoning proof

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:33.046
plays an outsized role in mathematics.

00:01:33.046 --> 00:01:37.225
So a general theory of inference
has to cover mathematics,

00:01:37.225 --> 00:01:40.890
as well as other kinds of reasoning,

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:43.410
you know, scientific
reasoning, everyday reasoning,

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:46.023
legal reasoning and so on.

00:01:47.296 --> 00:01:52.296
So lemme give you an
example from epistemology,

00:01:56.676 --> 00:01:59.400
Alvin Goldman in the late 1960s

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:03.778
came up with this sort of
short-lived account of the,

00:02:03.778 --> 00:02:06.030
of knowledge, a theory of knowledge,

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:07.920
which stated that you know something,

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:09.360
if it's true, you believe it,

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:13.650
and you are causally related
to the fact in the right way.

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:16.800
Now this theory was not meant
to apply to mathematics.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:18.000
It didn't apply to mathematics

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.835
because mathematics, as I just
said, is not a causal domain.

00:02:21.835 --> 00:02:24.960
A theory of inference, if it's gonna be,

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:25.890
suppose you want to come up

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:28.200
with a general theory of inference,

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:29.580
should you have that ambition,

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:31.620
it's gonna have to cover mathematics,

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:35.342
which as I said is
unusual in these respects.

00:02:35.342 --> 00:02:38.670
So just as a causal theory of knowledge

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:42.090
could never have been a fully
general account of knowledge,

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:43.830
a theory of inference,

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:46.870
which is just limited
to the causal inference

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:48.660
or to the physical case

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:52.261
is not gonna be a complete
theory of inference.

00:02:52.261 --> 00:02:54.780
Oh, it went the wrong way there.

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:55.980
Okay.

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:56.910
Or did I go the right way?

00:02:56.910 --> 00:02:58.680
I went the right way.

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:01.206
Okay, so like any other field, mathematics

00:03:01.206 --> 00:03:04.530
exhibits two very broad
types of inference, right.

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:07.170
So this is just an obvious point,

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:09.120
you can divide it into deductive inference

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:11.663
where the conclusion logically
follows from the premises

00:03:11.663 --> 00:03:14.992
or non-productive
inference where it doesn't.

00:03:14.992 --> 00:03:17.221
And this is just a non-productive,

00:03:17.221 --> 00:03:20.010
inference is just a catch-all category

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:22.383
that anything that's not deductive.

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:26.100
And I'm gonna take a closer
look at each with a sort of nod

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:29.730
to my research as prompted
by the organizers.

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:32.276
So what is deductive inference?

00:03:32.276 --> 00:03:35.073
Well, premises necessitate the conclusion,

00:03:35.073 --> 00:03:36.960
necessarily if the premises are true,

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:39.960
the conclusion also has to
be true, the starting points,

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:41.190
if the starting points are true,

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:43.114
then the conclusion will have to be true.

00:03:43.114 --> 00:03:45.078
And our best theory of logical implication

00:03:45.078 --> 00:03:47.100
or logical consequence adds something

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:50.292
to this necessation idea.

00:03:50.292 --> 00:03:52.782
It takes the necessation to be true

00:03:52.782 --> 00:03:55.800
to hold in virtue of form.

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:57.780
So here's a very simple
example of what I mean by that,

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:00.482
Ann is either American
or Brazilian A or B,

00:04:00.482 --> 00:04:01.878
that's the form.

00:04:01.878 --> 00:04:05.430
Anna, sorry, is not American, not A,

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:07.486
therefore Anna is Brazilian.

00:04:07.486 --> 00:04:11.610
Okay, so there the premises,
if the premises are true,

00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:14.040
the conclusion has to be true,

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:16.680
but not just that, it's in virtue of form.

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:18.552
Any argument that has that form

00:04:18.552 --> 00:04:23.552
is also gonna have that
property, the premises,

00:04:23.641 --> 00:04:25.530
well, the truth of the premises

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:28.777
necessitates that of the conclusion.

00:04:28.777 --> 00:04:30.380
So a very natural thought

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:35.380
is that deductive inference
is a logical implication.

00:04:35.442 --> 00:04:37.103
Now I've stated that very roughly,

00:04:37.103 --> 00:04:39.860
and that sort of breaks
down to two halves.

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:43.449
The first half is that
when we infer deductively,

00:04:43.449 --> 00:04:46.140
we move from some premise to,

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:48.155
or some premises to a conclusion

00:04:48.155 --> 00:04:51.260
that these premises logically imply.

00:04:51.260 --> 00:04:52.898
Okay, and that seems correct.

00:04:52.898 --> 00:04:56.880
But the second, so I
put a tick next to it,

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:01.880
but the second half, the other
direction is not so clear.

00:05:01.950 --> 00:05:03.678
In fact, I think it does not hold.

00:05:03.678 --> 00:05:05.250
The second direction,

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:08.281
is that if some premises
logically imply a conclusion,

00:05:08.281 --> 00:05:12.060
we can deductively infer the
conclusion from the premises.

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:13.980
Now there's one sense in which that's true

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:18.980
because you know, if you
can assume that you can,

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:23.879
you assume we have all
the powers that we need

00:05:23.879 --> 00:05:26.760
to follow a logical implication,

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:28.391
then, and it holds.

00:05:28.391 --> 00:05:32.670
But if you're not talking
about idealized versions of us,

00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:34.530
but you know, human beings,

00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:36.767
even just mildly idealized versions,

00:05:36.767 --> 00:05:40.470
then that second equation
is not gonna hold.

00:05:40.470 --> 00:05:43.545
And more importantly, we
need to sort of distinguish

00:05:43.545 --> 00:05:46.290
between implication on the one hand,

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:48.720
which is a relation between statements.

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:51.750
And that's not an anthropocentric notion,

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:52.583
that's just

00:05:53.999 --> 00:05:57.838
what holds a property
that some statements have

00:05:57.838 --> 00:06:00.960
and collections of statements
have and others don't.

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:03.630
Some premises entail a
conclusion or they do not.

00:06:03.630 --> 00:06:04.800
And there's inference,

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:06.900
which is something that an agent performs,

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:08.636
and which is anthropocentric
by definition,

00:06:08.636 --> 00:06:11.457
even if we consider idealized agents.

00:06:11.457 --> 00:06:14.100
So here's a kind of general question,

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:15.330
so what is logic?

00:06:15.330 --> 00:06:18.291
Well, implication is the
realm of logic proper.

00:06:18.291 --> 00:06:19.894
Right, implication,

00:06:19.894 --> 00:06:23.340
the relationship that some
statements have to others

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:25.080
whereby they logically imply them,

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:28.458
like in the Anna example.

00:06:28.458 --> 00:06:32.190
That's what logic is interested in.

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:34.143
Logic is interested in implication.

00:06:34.143 --> 00:06:37.263
And what is that best
theory of implication,

00:06:37.263 --> 00:06:39.450
by which I just mean?

00:06:39.450 --> 00:06:42.217
I use that interchangeably
with logical consequence.

00:06:42.217 --> 00:06:46.410
Well, in this, in the book
that Owen Griffiss and I wrote

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:47.243
that came out last year,

00:06:47.243 --> 00:06:48.810
we argued that there's a correct logic

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:50.963
and that it's highly infinitry.

00:06:50.963 --> 00:06:52.170
Okay.

00:06:52.170 --> 00:06:53.370
So what does that mean?

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:55.350
Well, I'm not gonna go
into too much detail,

00:06:55.350 --> 00:06:58.380
but one of the things it means is that

00:06:58.380 --> 00:07:00.480
you can conjoin infinitely many statements

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:03.390
or disjoin them, standard logic,

00:07:03.390 --> 00:07:06.728
you can only conjoin or
disjoin finite many statements.

00:07:06.728 --> 00:07:09.030
And you can also, in infinitry logic,

00:07:09.030 --> 00:07:10.320
you can also quantify over

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:12.333
infinitely many variables if you like.

00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:15.510
Unlike a natural language such as English,

00:07:15.510 --> 00:07:17.888
where sentences are a finite length.

00:07:17.888 --> 00:07:20.850
And this actually builds
on work by a Alfred Tarski

00:07:20.850 --> 00:07:22.653
and (indistinct), who's up next.

00:07:24.359 --> 00:07:26.460
So the point here is that if you,

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:28.140
once you understand the difference between

00:07:28.140 --> 00:07:31.290
implication and inference,
that sets you free,

00:07:31.290 --> 00:07:35.104
it enables you to give a
count of logical implication

00:07:35.104 --> 00:07:37.811
that isn't constrained by agents.

00:07:37.811 --> 00:07:40.350
Of course, the only reason
we got interested in logic

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:44.580
was because we were able
to perform inferences.

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:47.538
But once you realize that logic
is not about what we can do,

00:07:47.538 --> 00:07:50.512
but is about what follows from what,

00:07:50.512 --> 00:07:54.900
then you can, for example consider whether

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:58.121
logic is infinitry, which I believe it is.

00:07:58.121 --> 00:08:00.710
Now the logic of
mathematical inference is,

00:08:00.710 --> 00:08:03.003
can call that your home
logic, if you like,

00:08:04.351 --> 00:08:06.579
your home logic in a particular context,

00:08:06.579 --> 00:08:09.717
which you use for deductive
inference in that context

00:08:09.717 --> 00:08:12.540
is gonna have to be a sub
logic of the correct logic,

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:17.044
whatever that is, if you're
gonna reason correctly, okay.

00:08:17.044 --> 00:08:19.762
And a home logic is an epistemic tool,

00:08:19.762 --> 00:08:23.295
it's gonna be sort of
much more down to earth.

00:08:23.295 --> 00:08:25.880
It might, for example,
be first order logic,

00:08:25.880 --> 00:08:28.020
otherwise known as predicate logic.

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:28.980
So for those who don't know,

00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:32.100
first order logic has
names A, B, C, so on,

00:08:32.100 --> 00:08:36.060
it has variables X, Y,
Z, predicates, F, G, H,

00:08:36.060 --> 00:08:37.618
Boolean connectives,

00:08:37.618 --> 00:08:41.600
such as negation,
disjunction, conjunction,

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:45.365
and it has quantifies for all they exist.

00:08:45.365 --> 00:08:48.396
And all its sentences are a finite plan.

00:08:48.396 --> 00:08:51.721
And this is a very
standard logic of course.

00:08:51.721 --> 00:08:52.890
And your home logic

00:08:52.890 --> 00:08:55.140
is gonna depend on what
you're using it for, right?

00:08:55.140 --> 00:08:57.090
So you might even use
just propositional logic,

00:08:57.090 --> 00:08:59.133
which is just Boolean connectives.

00:09:00.690 --> 00:09:02.700
So much mathematical reasoning

00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:04.800
can in fact be modeled
by first order logic,

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:07.680
and not all of it, but a
lot of it can be modeled,

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:09.420
and magicians have pretty good

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:11.040
over the past century come up

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:14.940
with some good pretty good
models of mathematical inference

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:18.720
using first order logic, as
that's to say as reasoning

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:20.610
from first order stateable axioms

00:09:20.610 --> 00:09:22.950
via first order logic appropriate rules

00:09:22.950 --> 00:09:25.110
to first order stateable theorems.

00:09:25.110 --> 00:09:26.610
Okay.

00:09:26.610 --> 00:09:28.860
So you've gotta distinguish
the logic that you use

00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:30.990
as a epistemic tool for inference

00:09:30.990 --> 00:09:34.380
from the sort of correct
logic, which might be,

00:09:34.380 --> 00:09:38.544
the home logic might be
just the tip of the iceberg

00:09:38.544 --> 00:09:40.829
in a particular context.

00:09:40.829 --> 00:09:43.380
Okay, now I wanna move
on to something else.

00:09:43.380 --> 00:09:44.853
Setting aside the logic,

00:09:45.695 --> 00:09:48.920
talked about the logic
and mathematical inference

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:51.925
in my overview of mathematical inference

00:09:51.925 --> 00:09:54.330
that I'm giving you
inference and mathematics,

00:09:54.330 --> 00:09:56.531
how should we characterize
the epistemology

00:09:56.531 --> 00:09:59.143
of deductive mathematical inference?

00:09:59.143 --> 00:10:02.018
What are axioms

00:10:02.018 --> 00:10:04.681
and where do they come from
and how do we know them?

00:10:04.681 --> 00:10:08.850
A traditional picture here
is the Euclidean paradigm,

00:10:08.850 --> 00:10:12.611
okay, it's inspired by Euclid's
elements from about 300 BC,

00:10:12.611 --> 00:10:15.341
and it sort of held sway for more,

00:10:15.341 --> 00:10:17.583
you know, couple of millennia,

00:10:18.437 --> 00:10:21.810
the elements being a synthesis
of ancient Greek geometry

00:10:21.810 --> 00:10:23.747
and mathematics more generally.

00:10:23.747 --> 00:10:26.880
And very roughly the idea is
in mathematics, what do you do?

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:28.796
You start from self-evident axioms

00:10:28.796 --> 00:10:31.620
via evidence preserving rules,

00:10:31.620 --> 00:10:33.900
and you thereby infer some theorems,

00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:36.365
and you transfer the kind of evidence,

00:10:36.365 --> 00:10:38.370
this kind of maximal degree of evidence

00:10:38.370 --> 00:10:40.743
that the axioms have down to the theorems.

00:10:41.790 --> 00:10:44.730
And as I said, this was
a very prevalent picture

00:10:44.730 --> 00:10:46.469
of mathematical reasoning from Aristotle,

00:10:46.469 --> 00:10:48.300
at least from Aristotle,

00:10:48.300 --> 00:10:51.586
who actually comes a bit before Euclid

00:10:51.586 --> 00:10:54.990
to the 17th century and beyond.

00:10:54.990 --> 00:10:55.823
And there's a kind of,

00:10:55.823 --> 00:10:59.174
in the 17th century, there's
a kind of Euclid mania.

00:10:59.174 --> 00:11:02.460
A lot of people write books with a title,

00:11:02.460 --> 00:11:04.374
the Euclid of this or
the elements of that.

00:11:04.374 --> 00:11:08.181
Any form of knowledge has to be recast

00:11:08.181 --> 00:11:12.243
in this kind of Euclidean
form for it to be respectable.

00:11:13.811 --> 00:11:15.630
And today, Euclidean is

00:11:15.630 --> 00:11:19.800
as a kind of paradigm of
mathematical deductive inference

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:20.850
is on the wane,

00:11:20.850 --> 00:11:23.520
but it had some proponents
even though the 20th century,

00:11:23.520 --> 00:11:26.667
and there's some people that
you might call near Euclideans.

00:11:26.667 --> 00:11:28.740
And so Wes Wrigley and
I have reconstructed

00:11:28.740 --> 00:11:31.590
and tried to assess this
Euclidean picture in this book

00:11:31.590 --> 00:11:33.534
that's gonna come out next month.

00:11:33.534 --> 00:11:35.481
So what is this Euclidean paradigm?

00:11:35.481 --> 00:11:38.962
It can be expressed, we think
in these seven principles,

00:11:38.962 --> 00:11:40.496
slightly simplified here,

00:11:40.496 --> 00:11:42.903
that all axioms and
theorems are true, okay.

00:11:44.340 --> 00:11:46.440
All axioms are maximally evident.

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:48.960
Maximal evidence is preserved

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:51.550
when you reason from
premises to conclusion.

00:11:51.550 --> 00:11:53.280
As a find in this condition,

00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:55.647
the axioms have gotta be finitely many.

00:11:55.647 --> 00:11:58.236
The axioms have to be
general propositions.

00:11:58.236 --> 00:12:01.691
As an independence condition

00:12:01.691 --> 00:12:03.840
that the axioms have to be independent,

00:12:03.840 --> 00:12:05.430
each axiom has to be
independent of the others,

00:12:05.430 --> 00:12:08.550
that's to say if you had an axiom

00:12:08.550 --> 00:12:10.560
that you could deduce from the others,

00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:13.082
then it would be a theorem, not an axiom.

00:12:13.082 --> 00:12:17.103
Then it loses it's axiomatic status.

00:12:18.050 --> 00:12:21.030
There's no need to status
it as an independent axiom

00:12:21.030 --> 00:12:23.220
and a completeness condition,

00:12:23.220 --> 00:12:24.990
which is a kind of slightly schematic,

00:12:24.990 --> 00:12:26.370
all truths of a certain kind

00:12:26.370 --> 00:12:27.510
can be inferred from the axioms.

00:12:27.510 --> 00:12:31.164
If you are axiomatizing
geometry and you've emitted

00:12:31.164 --> 00:12:34.440
and you're not able to
prove a particular fact

00:12:34.440 --> 00:12:37.890
that you already believe
about triangle, say,

00:12:37.890 --> 00:12:41.370
then you need to
supplement your axiom set.

00:12:41.370 --> 00:12:42.620
You're missing something.

00:12:43.455 --> 00:12:46.691
This Euclidean picture no longer holds

00:12:46.691 --> 00:12:49.723
in full generality
about mathematics today.

00:12:49.723 --> 00:12:51.120
Well, truth still holds,

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:52.979
we still think our axioms

00:12:52.979 --> 00:12:56.220
should be true of their
intended subject matter.

00:12:56.220 --> 00:13:01.220
But truth here mostly takes
the form of stipulative truth.

00:13:01.266 --> 00:13:02.099
Okay.

00:13:03.013 --> 00:13:04.903
Think of the axioms of group theory.

00:13:04.903 --> 00:13:08.340
The axioms of group theory
define what it is to be a group.

00:13:08.340 --> 00:13:11.040
So they are true of groups,
but by kind of definition,

00:13:12.088 --> 00:13:13.924
by stipulation.

00:13:13.924 --> 00:13:17.130
If you think that one of
the axioms of group theory

00:13:17.130 --> 00:13:18.660
isn't true, you've just
kind of misunderstood

00:13:18.660 --> 00:13:20.362
what it is to be a group.

00:13:20.362 --> 00:13:23.820
The self-evidence principle
doesn't apply generally

00:13:23.820 --> 00:13:26.670
because in foundational
theories of mathematics,

00:13:26.670 --> 00:13:27.630
such as set theory,

00:13:27.630 --> 00:13:31.029
there are certainly some axioms
that are not self-evident.

00:13:31.029 --> 00:13:35.497
The flow principle fails
for sort of general reasons.

00:13:35.497 --> 00:13:38.530
Finiteness and generality usually apply,

00:13:38.530 --> 00:13:41.242
but independence does not apply generally.

00:13:41.242 --> 00:13:44.398
In standard axiomatic systems,

00:13:44.398 --> 00:13:47.303
there may be some
dependency among the axioms,

00:13:47.303 --> 00:13:51.210
for example, in the standards,
system of set theory,

00:13:51.210 --> 00:13:53.580
there is some dependence
between the axioms

00:13:53.580 --> 00:13:57.514
because, you know, other
criteria such as simplicity

00:13:57.514 --> 00:13:59.403
and ease of comparison,

00:14:00.328 --> 00:14:04.840
there a that kind of
override this independence

00:14:06.337 --> 00:14:07.834
(indistinct).

00:14:07.834 --> 00:14:12.150
And completeness fails we
now know for various reasons,

00:14:12.150 --> 00:14:12.983
it sort of depends

00:14:12.983 --> 00:14:15.540
on the version of
completeness that you go for,

00:14:15.540 --> 00:14:18.284
but if you go for the sort of
highest form of completeness

00:14:18.284 --> 00:14:23.284
where you can deduce either
a proposition or its negation

00:14:23.581 --> 00:14:26.550
in a kind of a system other than a sort of

00:14:26.550 --> 00:14:30.201
very simple axiomatic
systems, Godel's theorem,

00:14:30.201 --> 00:14:33.420
Godel's incompleteness
theorem is gonna tell you that

00:14:33.420 --> 00:14:34.970
completeness is not gonna help.

00:14:36.630 --> 00:14:38.493
So what should replace Euclideanism?

00:14:39.420 --> 00:14:41.878
Well, the kind of move
away from Euclideanism

00:14:41.878 --> 00:14:44.550
introduces a couple of ideas,

00:14:44.550 --> 00:14:47.640
first, the idea is instead of
talking about self-evidence,

00:14:47.640 --> 00:14:49.489
think about intrinsic plausibility

00:14:49.489 --> 00:14:52.410
or justification on a conception.

00:14:52.410 --> 00:14:55.950
If you think of the structure
of the natural numbers,

00:14:55.950 --> 00:14:58.230
you have a certain conception
of the natural numbers,

00:14:58.230 --> 00:15:00.084
then principles can seem

00:15:00.084 --> 00:15:04.529
plausible on that conception
or not, likewise with sets.

00:15:04.529 --> 00:15:07.260
And this actually, and
notice that this allows,

00:15:07.260 --> 00:15:12.180
once you move from self-evidence
to intrinsic plausibility

00:15:12.180 --> 00:15:14.245
or justification on a conception,

00:15:14.245 --> 00:15:17.640
intrinsic plausibility on
a conception I should say,

00:15:17.640 --> 00:15:22.640
you thereby allow for degrees
of plausibility as well.

00:15:22.650 --> 00:15:24.570
And also extrinsic justification

00:15:24.570 --> 00:15:26.220
should also be part of the mix.

00:15:26.220 --> 00:15:28.830
So here's where pure mathematics

00:15:28.830 --> 00:15:31.695
starts to look more like science.

00:15:31.695 --> 00:15:33.810
Extrinsic justification

00:15:33.810 --> 00:15:36.480
can be sort of divided
into two broad categories,

00:15:36.480 --> 00:15:38.460
one is regressive.

00:15:38.460 --> 00:15:41.340
So this is analogous to
the kind of justification

00:15:41.340 --> 00:15:43.950
that's enjoyed by scientific postulate,

00:15:43.950 --> 00:15:44.783
which predicts the data.

00:15:44.783 --> 00:15:46.170
You've got some sort of mathematical data

00:15:46.170 --> 00:15:51.170
in the mathematical case that
could be very simple claims,

00:15:51.180 --> 00:15:53.160
you know, simple arithmetic
like two plus three equals five,

00:15:53.160 --> 00:15:55.360
where it could be more
sophisticated claims.

00:15:59.138 --> 00:16:03.240
And the idea is that an
axiom gets to earn its keep

00:16:03.240 --> 00:16:06.990
by predicting some of these claims,

00:16:06.990 --> 00:16:09.001
just as in those kind of scientific case.

00:16:09.001 --> 00:16:13.592
But there's a theoretical form
of extrinsic justification

00:16:13.592 --> 00:16:15.810
also exists and takes the following form,

00:16:15.810 --> 00:16:18.600
that principles can enhance the simplicity

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:23.298
and economy and unifying
power of the overall theory.

00:16:23.298 --> 00:16:27.003
And thereby, I'll kind of
justify it in that way too.

00:16:28.268 --> 00:16:31.350
Okay, so talked about mathematics

00:16:31.350 --> 00:16:32.310
and general theory of inference

00:16:32.310 --> 00:16:34.327
and about deductive inference

00:16:34.327 --> 00:16:36.818
and mathematics focusing on the logic,

00:16:36.818 --> 00:16:40.260
and also on the axioms and
what justifies the axioms

00:16:40.260 --> 00:16:42.390
and kind of the old picture

00:16:42.390 --> 00:16:44.493
versus the new one that has emerged.

00:16:46.110 --> 00:16:47.340
I want to end by talking about

00:16:47.340 --> 00:16:49.620
non deductive inference in mathematics.

00:16:49.620 --> 00:16:50.550
Okay.

00:16:50.550 --> 00:16:52.500
So remember that non deductive inference

00:16:52.500 --> 00:16:54.090
is a catch-all category

00:16:54.090 --> 00:16:55.827
that encompasses any sort of inference,

00:16:55.827 --> 00:16:57.213
and that's not deductive.

00:16:59.158 --> 00:17:01.740
So there's some broad categories here,

00:17:01.740 --> 00:17:03.720
for example, numerative induction,

00:17:03.720 --> 00:17:05.384
sometimes people just say induction

00:17:05.384 --> 00:17:08.190
when you infer from all observed A's,

00:17:08.190 --> 00:17:10.920
having been B's in a very simple case

00:17:10.920 --> 00:17:15.444
that all A's are being B's
from the observed cases of A's

00:17:15.444 --> 00:17:19.050
having some property
to the unobserved ones

00:17:19.050 --> 00:17:20.860
also having that property.

00:17:20.860 --> 00:17:22.909
Or there's inference
of the best explanation

00:17:22.909 --> 00:17:24.243
and very schematically

00:17:24.243 --> 00:17:28.288
as a body of established
facts to be explained.

00:17:28.288 --> 00:17:30.990
And these facts can be a
mixture of observations

00:17:30.990 --> 00:17:32.354
and more theoretical claims,

00:17:32.354 --> 00:17:35.010
and among the range of explanations,

00:17:35.010 --> 00:17:36.750
except you should accept the best one,

00:17:36.750 --> 00:17:39.420
that's what inference of the
best explanation looks like,

00:17:39.420 --> 00:17:40.570
and it's not deductive.

00:17:41.445 --> 00:17:42.507
Okay.

00:17:42.507 --> 00:17:44.306
There's a caveat here about the

00:17:44.306 --> 00:17:46.353
explanation having to be a good one.

00:17:47.700 --> 00:17:48.900
So what about mathematics?

00:17:48.900 --> 00:17:49.950
I mean mathematics,

00:17:49.950 --> 00:17:53.273
how does mathematics rely
on non-deductive reasoning?

00:17:53.273 --> 00:17:56.189
Well, there's some obvious
ways in which it does so, yeah.

00:17:56.189 --> 00:17:57.540
I mean, for a start,

00:17:57.540 --> 00:18:00.490
the choice of research
project, for example.

00:18:00.490 --> 00:18:04.378
You should choose a research
project that's tractable

00:18:04.378 --> 00:18:08.111
either a research project
for you or your student.

00:18:08.111 --> 00:18:12.330
And by definition, the project
has the research project,

00:18:12.330 --> 00:18:17.157
you haven't improved what
you might wanna prove.

00:18:17.157 --> 00:18:19.230
Therefore, in the absence of a proof,

00:18:19.230 --> 00:18:20.460
you've gotta kind of make a judgment

00:18:20.460 --> 00:18:22.771
about whether it's
attractable project or not.

00:18:22.771 --> 00:18:26.084
There's also what philosophers
called testimonial knowledge,

00:18:26.084 --> 00:18:27.840
a justification.

00:18:27.840 --> 00:18:30.995
So just like any other sphere of knowledge

00:18:30.995 --> 00:18:32.790
outside mathematics.

00:18:32.790 --> 00:18:35.010
Fermat's has last theorem, for example,

00:18:35.010 --> 00:18:39.896
approved by Wiles Taylor in the mid 1990s

00:18:39.896 --> 00:18:42.381
is a 100 page paper.

00:18:42.381 --> 00:18:46.800
Only experts in the field can
read it and understand it,

00:18:46.800 --> 00:18:49.845
but you and I know that it's true

00:18:49.845 --> 00:18:53.566
because the experts are a
kind of reliable community

00:18:53.566 --> 00:18:57.621
and therefore that knowledge
can be passed on to us.

00:18:57.621 --> 00:19:01.890
And in fact, most mathematical
knowledge is of this kind,

00:19:01.890 --> 00:19:05.653
you know, whether it's
sophisticated claims, proofs of,

00:19:06.638 --> 00:19:09.720
such as the proof of Fermat's last theorem

00:19:09.720 --> 00:19:12.354
or just simple ones for the
sort of lame mathematician

00:19:12.354 --> 00:19:14.452
who knows Pythagoras's theorem

00:19:14.452 --> 00:19:17.720
because that teacher told them it was true

00:19:17.720 --> 00:19:19.620
or the textbook told them it was true.

00:19:20.583 --> 00:19:22.800
There's also computer generator,

00:19:22.800 --> 00:19:24.870
computer assisted,
computer verified proofs.

00:19:24.870 --> 00:19:27.300
Really, this is just an
example of the former

00:19:27.300 --> 00:19:32.300
because you're assuming
that the computer is,

00:19:32.906 --> 00:19:35.910
the hardware the software is reliable

00:19:35.910 --> 00:19:39.840
when you're relying on it
to generate some result

00:19:39.840 --> 00:19:42.990
or some calculation.

00:19:42.990 --> 00:19:46.500
And so 21st century mathematics
will be severely hamstrung

00:19:46.500 --> 00:19:49.380
by a strict insistence on
deductive reasoning only.

00:19:49.380 --> 00:19:52.623
I mean, this would be a kind
of sort of proof fanaticism.

00:19:53.812 --> 00:19:58.016
Is there non deductive
knowledge in mathematics?

00:19:58.016 --> 00:19:58.910
Okay.

00:19:58.910 --> 00:20:02.888
By which I mean knowledge of
a mathematical proposition

00:20:02.888 --> 00:20:05.910
without anyone having a
proof of it, not just,

00:20:05.910 --> 00:20:09.120
you know, not even Wiles and Taylor,

00:20:09.120 --> 00:20:12.755
but no one whatsoever, no
computer having come up with it.

00:20:12.755 --> 00:20:17.755
So I take the sort of radical view

00:20:18.429 --> 00:20:21.450
that you can know a proposition

00:20:21.450 --> 00:20:23.190
without anyone having proved it.

00:20:23.190 --> 00:20:26.230
So mathematicians don't like this at all.

00:20:26.230 --> 00:20:28.710
In fact, mathematicians use the word

00:20:28.710 --> 00:20:32.309
no in a mathematical
context synonymously with

00:20:32.309 --> 00:20:35.430
it's been proved or we have proved.

00:20:35.430 --> 00:20:39.330
So we've known since
2002, then such and such

00:20:39.330 --> 00:20:41.193
because someone proved it in 2002.

00:20:42.090 --> 00:20:43.238
But is that right?

00:20:43.238 --> 00:20:47.310
So take an example from number theory,

00:20:47.310 --> 00:20:49.525
Goldbach's conjecture

00:20:49.525 --> 00:20:53.190
or the example of primarily
testing where you test the,

00:20:53.190 --> 00:20:55.004
whether a number is prime

00:20:55.004 --> 00:20:58.950
and then you can quickly convince yourself

00:20:58.950 --> 00:21:01.799
with probability that with
probability one minus epsilon,

00:21:01.799 --> 00:21:04.429
where epsilon is really
tiny, that it is prime.

00:21:04.429 --> 00:21:07.620
So Goldbach's conjecture
states that every even number

00:21:07.620 --> 00:21:10.777
is a sum of two primes, every
even number bigger than two.

00:21:10.777 --> 00:21:14.714
So there's a lot of
enumerative evidence behind it.

00:21:14.714 --> 00:21:18.270
Four times 10 to the 18
cases have been tested.

00:21:18.270 --> 00:21:20.160
There's also evidence
that the number of ways

00:21:20.160 --> 00:21:23.424
of writing an even number
is a sum of two primes grows

00:21:23.424 --> 00:21:27.420
with that number, not in a
kind of linear or strict way,

00:21:27.420 --> 00:21:30.990
but as an overall increasing trend.

00:21:30.990 --> 00:21:33.390
That proves some very
slightly weaker theorems.

00:21:33.390 --> 00:21:34.890
There's heuristic arguments

00:21:34.890 --> 00:21:39.510
about the distribution of
the primes that don't prove

00:21:39.510 --> 00:21:41.550
but suggest that the argument is true,

00:21:41.550 --> 00:21:43.791
but that the Goldbach's
conjecture is true.

00:21:43.791 --> 00:21:47.130
And there are also analogs
of Goldbach's conjecture

00:21:47.130 --> 00:21:49.230
that hold in random models of the primes.

00:21:49.230 --> 00:21:52.830
So the probability that
a number N is prime

00:21:52.830 --> 00:21:53.823
is one over log-n.

00:21:55.040 --> 00:22:00.040
And if you generate sequences of numbers

00:22:00.088 --> 00:22:02.472
with that probability,

00:22:02.472 --> 00:22:04.800
that's to say that number
n appears on the list

00:22:04.800 --> 00:22:07.239
with probability one over n,

00:22:07.239 --> 00:22:09.979
you get a kind of prime-like sequence,

00:22:09.979 --> 00:22:12.090
and then you can have an analog

00:22:12.090 --> 00:22:14.490
of Goldbach's conjecture for that.

00:22:14.490 --> 00:22:17.593
And you know, a version of
Goldbach's conjecture holds

00:22:17.593 --> 00:22:21.663
with probability one for these sequences.

00:22:22.500 --> 00:22:24.420
It doesn't prove Goldbach's conjecture,

00:22:24.420 --> 00:22:27.681
but it does tell you

00:22:27.681 --> 00:22:31.774
what the class of those
sequences looks like.

00:22:31.774 --> 00:22:35.250
Also, you could come up with
sort of philosophical arguments

00:22:35.250 --> 00:22:38.040
to diffuse objections to this kind of idea

00:22:38.040 --> 00:22:40.466
that you can know mathematical
claims without proof.

00:22:40.466 --> 00:22:41.700
And one such argument

00:22:41.700 --> 00:22:44.790
is based on philosophical
analysis of knowledge.

00:22:44.790 --> 00:22:46.753
So I mentioned one
before, the causal theory.

00:22:46.753 --> 00:22:49.923
And as I said, that one
doesn't apply to mathematics.

00:22:50.780 --> 00:22:54.471
Or to put it positively,
it doesn't rule out

00:22:54.471 --> 00:22:57.885
non-deductive knowledge
of mathematics either.

00:22:57.885 --> 00:22:59.937
And if you look at the kind of,

00:22:59.937 --> 00:23:01.890
the philosophical analysis of knowledge,

00:23:01.890 --> 00:23:03.720
the things that have got
some sort of traction

00:23:03.720 --> 00:23:05.820
in the literature over
the last sort of 50 years,

00:23:05.820 --> 00:23:10.025
the 60 years that philosophers
have been thinking about this

00:23:10.025 --> 00:23:13.410
in all detail, of course they
did think about it before.

00:23:13.410 --> 00:23:14.970
But they sort of fall into two camps,

00:23:14.970 --> 00:23:17.361
either they don't apply to mathematics

00:23:17.361 --> 00:23:20.130
like the causal theory, or if they do,

00:23:20.130 --> 00:23:22.800
they allow non-deductive
knowledge of mathematics,

00:23:22.800 --> 00:23:24.930
for example, the classic case

00:23:24.930 --> 00:23:27.780
of knowledge being justified true belief.

00:23:27.780 --> 00:23:30.750
Not you can have
non-deductive justification

00:23:30.750 --> 00:23:32.750
as in the case of Goldbach's conjecture.

00:23:34.267 --> 00:23:36.120
And actually, if you think about it,

00:23:36.120 --> 00:23:37.590
there's sort of non deductive reasoning

00:23:37.590 --> 00:23:41.670
that's at the heart of deduction,
because as I said earlier,

00:23:41.670 --> 00:23:45.187
axions are at least in part
extrinsically justified.

00:23:45.187 --> 00:23:49.087
And this is a form of
non-deductive justification, right.

00:23:49.087 --> 00:23:52.680
If you believe something

00:23:52.680 --> 00:23:55.470
in part because it has
some nice implications

00:23:55.470 --> 00:23:56.940
that you already believe,

00:23:56.940 --> 00:23:59.818
then you're not deducing
it from some axioms,

00:23:59.818 --> 00:24:02.490
that form of justification
is non-deductive.

00:24:02.490 --> 00:24:07.490
And so abandoning this Euclidean
picture helps undermine

00:24:07.562 --> 00:24:09.990
the sort of specialness
of deductive reasoning.

00:24:09.990 --> 00:24:13.200
If there's some sort of
non deductive justification

00:24:13.200 --> 00:24:14.548
behind some of the axioms,

00:24:14.548 --> 00:24:18.960
then the kind of what's special
about deductive reasoning

00:24:18.960 --> 00:24:20.463
has been undermined.

00:24:21.420 --> 00:24:23.670
Another way in which there's
sort of non-deductive reasoning

00:24:23.670 --> 00:24:24.630
at the heart of deduction

00:24:24.630 --> 00:24:27.717
is that mathematicians rarely
give full proof, right?

00:24:27.717 --> 00:24:29.610
Especially expert mathematicians,

00:24:29.610 --> 00:24:32.233
they tend to give proof sketches.

00:24:32.233 --> 00:24:33.690
And so your confidence

00:24:33.690 --> 00:24:37.556
that a proof sketch can be
elaborated into full proof,

00:24:37.556 --> 00:24:40.036
where does that come from?

00:24:40.036 --> 00:24:42.671
If you give a proof sketch,

00:24:42.671 --> 00:24:45.477
where does your confidence come from?

00:24:45.477 --> 00:24:47.386
What does it depend on

00:24:47.386 --> 00:24:50.262
that it can actually be
turned into a foolproof?

00:24:50.262 --> 00:24:54.360
Well, just kind of your knowledge

00:24:54.360 --> 00:24:55.770
that this has worked in the past

00:24:55.770 --> 00:24:58.500
or that steps of the kind
of this size actually,

00:24:58.500 --> 00:24:59.400
if you spell them out,

00:24:59.400 --> 00:25:02.310
would actually be valid and so on.

00:25:02.310 --> 00:25:06.090
So that's justified non-deductively,

00:25:06.090 --> 00:25:08.690
that's just from your
experience as a mathematician.

00:25:09.540 --> 00:25:12.893
So lemme summarize the four
things that I've told you

00:25:12.893 --> 00:25:16.535
in my overview of
inference in mathematics,

00:25:16.535 --> 00:25:18.600
any general theory of inference

00:25:18.600 --> 00:25:21.900
should you wanna try and come up with one

00:25:21.900 --> 00:25:23.790
has to cover mathematics as well,

00:25:23.790 --> 00:25:26.214
which is unusual in several ways.

00:25:26.214 --> 00:25:29.460
The second thing is
that deductive inference

00:25:29.460 --> 00:25:31.380
isn't the same as logical implication.

00:25:31.380 --> 00:25:33.990
I take deductive inference to
be something an agent does,

00:25:33.990 --> 00:25:37.853
logical implication to be a
relation between statements.

00:25:37.853 --> 00:25:40.650
And I think, and you
don't have to buy this,

00:25:40.650 --> 00:25:44.287
but I think the latter is
this highly inflammatory.

00:25:44.287 --> 00:25:47.040
The third point was
that Euclidean paradigm

00:25:47.040 --> 00:25:49.410
no longer applies
wholesale to mathematics,

00:25:49.410 --> 00:25:51.660
a kind of a different
picture of mathematical,

00:25:51.660 --> 00:25:53.610
deductive mathematical justification

00:25:53.610 --> 00:25:55.574
is now much more current.

00:25:55.574 --> 00:26:00.360
And my fourth broad point was
that non-deductive reasoning

00:26:00.360 --> 00:26:01.708
is prevalent in mathematics,

00:26:01.708 --> 00:26:03.930
and is in perfectly good standing.

00:26:03.930 --> 00:26:05.133
Thank you.

00:26:05.133 --> 00:26:08.050
(class applauding)

00:26:10.926 --> 00:26:12.813
- [Speaker One] (indistinct) question.

00:26:12.813 --> 00:26:15.420
(indistinct speaking)

00:26:15.420 --> 00:26:17.460
- Thanks, this is a
really interesting talk.

00:26:17.460 --> 00:26:18.330
I was curious,

00:26:18.330 --> 00:26:20.970
just because you've characterized
as Euclidean program,

00:26:20.970 --> 00:26:23.720
do you think that there's
a central component

00:26:23.720 --> 00:26:25.410
of diagrammatic reasoning
that's a part of,

00:26:25.410 --> 00:26:28.170
I mean, at least as
Euclid originally did it,

00:26:28.170 --> 00:26:29.880
that something that's important?

00:26:29.880 --> 00:26:31.080
Because there's some people

00:26:31.080 --> 00:26:32.340
who I think wanna characterize that

00:26:32.340 --> 00:26:34.950
as possibly not being something

00:26:34.950 --> 00:26:37.050
you can regiment in first order of logic.

00:26:37.050 --> 00:26:38.310
So.

00:26:38.310 --> 00:26:40.372
- Yeah, thank you, that's a
really good question, yeah.

00:26:40.372 --> 00:26:42.813
So the Euclidean paradigm

00:26:42.813 --> 00:26:45.687
or the Euclidean foundationalism
as I described it

00:26:45.687 --> 00:26:48.445
didn't say anything about diagrams,

00:26:48.445 --> 00:26:51.903
but Euclid of course,
in the elements relies,

00:26:52.831 --> 00:26:57.831
very much relies on spatial
reasoning and on diagrams,

00:26:58.632 --> 00:27:02.173
diagrammatic reasoning.

00:27:02.173 --> 00:27:06.240
You can't really be convinced
of many of the proposition

00:27:06.240 --> 00:27:07.890
unless you draw a diagram for yourself

00:27:07.890 --> 00:27:09.678
or see it on the page.

00:27:09.678 --> 00:27:14.370
And Euclid was criticized
for that in the 19th century,

00:27:14.370 --> 00:27:16.050
and the late 19th century, especially

00:27:16.050 --> 00:27:18.572
as kind of modern logic
started to be developed,

00:27:18.572 --> 00:27:20.970
people realized that there
were all sorts of gaps

00:27:20.970 --> 00:27:22.274
in his reasoning.

00:27:22.274 --> 00:27:24.351
So a kind of a new ideal

00:27:24.351 --> 00:27:26.640
for mathematical reasoning was born

00:27:26.640 --> 00:27:29.257
whereby the steps in
mathematical reasoning

00:27:29.257 --> 00:27:33.154
are actually logical or deductively valid,

00:27:33.154 --> 00:27:38.100
and do not rely on diagrammatic
reasoning in any kind.

00:27:38.100 --> 00:27:39.810
But I think the Euclidean,

00:27:39.810 --> 00:27:43.473
the way I wanted to characterize
Euclidean foundationalism

00:27:43.473 --> 00:27:45.207
was broader than that.

00:27:45.207 --> 00:27:47.007
I think that's a very narrow way

00:27:47.007 --> 00:27:50.012
and a very kind of relatively modern way

00:27:50.012 --> 00:27:53.148
of characterizing mathematical reasoning.

00:27:53.148 --> 00:27:55.830
If you wanna take in mathematic,

00:27:55.830 --> 00:27:58.435
the history, much broader
sweep of mathematics

00:27:58.435 --> 00:28:02.351
and understand why the
Euclidean picture had

00:28:02.351 --> 00:28:04.950
was so important and what's so dominant,

00:28:04.950 --> 00:28:06.780
prevalent for so many centuries,

00:28:06.780 --> 00:28:11.310
I think you have to allow
steps in mathematical reasoning

00:28:11.310 --> 00:28:14.520
that could be guided by spatial intuition

00:28:14.520 --> 00:28:16.020
that are not strictly logical.

00:28:16.864 --> 00:28:19.293
When I describe mathematical inference,

00:28:20.232 --> 00:28:22.380
the deductive inference,

00:28:22.380 --> 00:28:24.150
that's a kind of a modern,

00:28:24.150 --> 00:28:26.350
a much more modern way
of thinking about it.

00:28:27.542 --> 00:28:29.748
- [Speaker One] I would like,

00:28:29.748 --> 00:28:34.333
should we move to T and we
can continue asking questions.

00:28:34.333 --> 00:28:35.575
(Alexander chuckles)
So we (indistinct)

00:28:35.575 --> 00:28:38.913
time, or maybe later in the day?

00:28:38.913 --> 00:28:40.710
So thank you.

00:28:40.710 --> 00:28:42.249
Thanks for having this talk.

00:28:42.249 --> 00:28:44.275
- All right.
(Audience applauding)

